          '**NPUAP Pressure Injury Staging**:\n\n- **Stage 1**: Non-blanchable erythema of intact skin. Skin is not broken. May be warmer/cooler, firmer/softer than surrounding tissue.\n- **Stage 2**: Partial-thickness skin loss involving epidermis and/or dermis. Presents as a shallow open ulcer with red-pink wound bed, or intact/ruptured blister. **No slough or granulation tissue**.\n- **Stage 3**: Full-thickness skin loss. Subcutaneous fat may be visible, but bone, tendon, and muscle are NOT exposed. May include undermining and tunneling.\n- **Stage 4**: Full-thickness tissue loss with **exposed bone, tendon, or muscle**. Slough or eschar may be present. Often includes undermining and tunneling. **This patient\'s wound is Stage 4** -- sacral bone is visible.\n- **Unstageable**: Full-thickness tissue loss with wound base obscured by slough (yellow, tan, gray, green, brown) or eschar (tan, brown, black). Cannot be staged until debrided.\n- **Deep Tissue Pressure Injury (DTPI)**: Intact or non-intact skin with localized area of persistent non-blanchable deep red, maroon, purple discoloration or epidermal separation revealing a dark wound bed. Damage originated at the bone-muscle interface.\n\n**Osteomyelitis Workup**:\nWith exposed sacral bone, purulent drainage, fever, and elevated inflammatory markers (WBC 14.2K, ESR 78, CRP 45), osteomyelitis must be ruled out:\n\n1. **MRI of the Sacrum** (with and without gadolinium): **Gold standard imaging** for osteomyelitis. Sensitivity 90%, specificity 80%. Findings: bone marrow edema (T1 low, T2/STIR high signal), cortical destruction, periosteal reaction, rim-enhancing fluid collections.\n\n2. **Bone Biopsy**: **Definitive diagnostic test**. Percutaneous or intraoperative bone biopsy with culture and histopathology. Surface wound cultures are unreliable (colonized with multiple organisms). Bone biopsy provides organism identification and antibiotic sensitivities.\n\n3. **Blood Cultures**: If systemic sepsis is suspected (fever, tachycardia).\n\n4. **Probe-to-Bone Test**: At bedside, insert a sterile metal probe into the wound. If bone is palpated (positive probe-to-bone), the **positive predictive value for osteomyelitis is 89%**. In this patient, bone is already visible, making osteomyelitis very likely.\n\n5. **Nuclear Medicine**: Labeled WBC scan (Indium-111 or Technetium-99m HMPAO) if MRI is contraindicated or equivocal. Triple-phase bone scan is sensitive but not specific (cannot distinguish infection from reactive changes).\n\n6. **Inflammatory Markers for Monitoring**: Serial ESR and CRP to track treatment response. Procalcitonin if bacteremia suspected.',
