pplementation; consider erythropoietin if anemia persists\n- Dietitian referral for meal planning and monitoring\n- Weekly weight checks; repeat albumin/prealbumin every 2 weeks\n\n**5. Pressure Offloading** (Most important modifiable factor):\n- **Complete bed rest** with pressure redistribution mattress (alternating pressure or low-air-loss mattress) during acute healing phase\n- **NO sitting on the wound** until significant healing has occurred (may take weeks to months)\n- Repositioning schedule: Turn every 2 hours; 30-degree lateral tilt positions\n- When sitting is gradually reintroduced: Start with 15 minutes, inspect wound, slowly increase\n- New wheelchair cushion: ROHO or JAY pressure-relieving cushion with pressure mapping evaluation\n\n**6. Surgical Reconstruction (Flap Surgery)**:\n- **Myocutaneous flap closure** (gluteal rotation flap or V-Y advancement flap) should be considered once:\n  - Osteomyelitis is adequately treated (minimum 6 weeks IV antibiotics)\n  - Wound bed is clean with healthy granulation tissue\n  - Nutritional status is optimized (albumin >3.0)\n  - Patient is committed to post-operative pressure relief protocol (minimum 6-8 weeks of strict bed rest post-flap)\n- Plastic surgery consultation for surgical planning\n- **Post-flap protocol**: 6-8 weeks strict bed rest, progressive sitting program (starting at 15 min, increase by 15 min/day as tolerated with wound checks)\n\n**7. Depression Treatment**:\n- PHQ-9 score of 19 indicates severe depression -- this is driving his self-neglect\n- Start **SSRI** (sertraline 50 mg daily -- first-line, favorable side effect profile in SCI)\n- Psychiatry or psychology referral for therapy\n- Address social isolation: SCI peer support, community reintegration\n- **This is not optional** -- untreated depression will undermine all wound healing efforts',
